topics=[]; cards=[]
def topic(id,title,subtitle,lesson,rows,kind='Course core'):
    topics.append(dict(id=id,title=title,subtitle=subtitle,lesson=lesson,kind=kind))
    for line in rows.strip().splitlines():
        p=line.split('|')
        assert len(p)>=7,line
        slug,title,q,a,why,w1,w2,*rest=p
        formula=rest[0] if rest else ''
        cards.append(dict(id=id+'-'+slug,topic=id,title=title,prompts=[q,'Explain '+title.lower()+' in your own words, and give an example.'],answer=a,explanation=why,choices=[a,w1,w2],formula=formula,source='coursera',kind=kind))
topic('purpose','01 · Why finance exists','Risk sharing, incentives & innovation',
'''A financial contract moves purchasing power across time and across possible future states. Equity shares uncertain business results; debt promises payments; insurance covers defined losses. Useful finance connects productive projects with savers and makes risks bearable. Ask three questions of any instrument: who receives which cash flows, in which states, and who bears the downside?\n\nContracts cannot fix every problem. Hidden information creates adverse selection; changed incentives create moral hazard; delegated decisions create agency problems. A contract that is sensible for one participant may still amplify system-wide risk. Shiller’s emphasis on democratization includes broader access to risk protection, not just easier speculation.''',
'''
claims|Contingent claims|What makes a financial claim contingent?|Its payoff depends on a future state or event.|A share, a fire policy and an option distribute money differently across future outcomes. Describing those states reveals the economic substance of the contract.|Its payout is always certain.|It must trade on an exchange.
sharing|Risk sharing|How can giving up expected income make a farmer better off?|Certainty can be worth more to a risk-averse farmer than the extra expected income.|Compare utility of wealth, not only the average dollar outcome. Pooling works best when different farmers do not all lose together.|Risk sharing must increase expected income.|Insurance removes all risk from society.
liability|Limited liability|What does limited liability usually mean for a common shareholder?|The shareholder ordinarily risks the amount invested, rather than personal assets beyond it.|The company can still fail and creditors can lose. Personal guarantees, fraud and other legal exceptions are separate issues.|The stock cannot lose value.|The government guarantees the shareholder’s investment.
adverse|Adverse selection|Why might a voluntarily insured pool contain unusually risky people?|People who know they face greater risk have more reason to buy coverage.|This is information hidden before contracting. Underwriting and broad participation can help, with tradeoffs for access.|Insurance necessarily makes people careless afterward.|Insurers always know every customer’s risk.
moral|Moral hazard|A fully insured owner stops maintaining safety equipment. What problem is this?|Protection changed behavior after the contract: moral hazard.|Deductibles, monitoring and shared losses can preserve incentives. Moral hazard need not involve deliberate wrongdoing.|Adverse selection before the policy.|A reduction in aggregate risk.
agency|Principal–agent problems|Why can a bonus tied to this year’s profits hurt a bank’s owners?|The manager may take delayed risks to improve short-term measured performance.|Good governance aligns incentives across time and across stakeholders. Compensation, audits and covenants reduce but do not erase agency costs.|Managers always bear all future losses.|High profits prove there are no hidden risks.
innovation|Financial innovation|What test should a useful financial innovation pass?|It should improve allocation or risk management after accounting for costs and fragility.|Complexity alone is not progress. A new instrument may improve access while also introducing leverage, opacity or correlated exposure.|It must make every participant richer.|It is useful whenever trading volume rises.
externality|Financial externalities|How can a rational forced asset sale harm other institutions?|It can depress shared asset prices and trigger more losses and sales.|A lender may protect itself by demanding collateral while contributing to a system-wide spiral. Private incentives can differ from social welfare.|The sale automatically destroys all contracts.|Only irrational decisions can create crises.
''')
topic('risk','02 · Risk & portfolios','Probability, correlation, CAPM & tails',
'''Start with outcomes and probabilities. Expected return is a weighted average, not a promise. Variance measures dispersion around that average; covariance measures joint movement. A portfolio’s exposure is a combination of its assets and their relationships. Diversification reduces company-specific risks but cannot guarantee protection from economy-wide shocks.\n\nMean–variance models simplify a richer problem: people have liabilities, labor income, liquidity needs and exposure to rare disasters. Historical estimates are uncertain. CAPM gives a benchmark for the price of market risk; it is not a complete empirical law. A stress scenario asks how the portfolio behaves when usual relationships break.''',
'''
expected|Expected return|Is an investment’s expected return a guaranteed outcome?|No: it is a probability-weighted average of possible returns.|A risky asset can have positive expected return and a substantial probability of loss. Estimates depend on the chosen distribution.|Yes, if enough investors buy it.|Yes, if the arithmetic is correct.
variance|Variance and volatility|What does return standard deviation measure?|Dispersion of returns around their mean.|Variance uses squared deviations; standard deviation restores return units. It counts positive surprises as well as negative ones and does not describe the entire tail.|Only permanent losses.|The average return itself.|Variance = Σ pᵢ(Rᵢ − μ)²; volatility = √variance
correlation|Correlation|Why can two volatile assets form a less volatile portfolio?|Their fluctuations may partially offset when correlation is below one.|The benefit depends on weights, volatility and joint movements. Correlations can change in stress; zero correlation does not imply independence in general.|Owning two assets guarantees no losses.|Their individual volatilities must fall.
systematic|Systematic risk|Can owning many technology shares eliminate recession risk?|No: diversification leaves common economic and sector exposures.|Idiosyncratic risk is company-specific; systematic risk is shared. Many names can still represent the same underlying bet.|Yes, once you own 100 shares.|Yes, provided every company is profitable.
beta|Beta|Does beta measure all of a stock’s risk?|No: it measures covariance with market returns relative to market variance.|A low-beta stock can have large idiosyncratic or tail risk. Estimated beta depends on the benchmark and measurement period.|Yes, beta equals total volatility.|No, beta measures only the dividend yield.|βᵢ = Cov(Rᵢ,Rₘ) / Var(Rₘ)
capm|CAPM|Which risk does the basic CAPM reward with higher expected returns?|Nondiversifiable market risk, measured by beta.|Its assumptions include mean–variance choice and a common market portfolio. Size, value and other empirical factors complicate the one-beta account.|All company-specific risk equally.|Only the chance of a positive return.|E[Rᵢ] = Rf + βᵢ(E[Rₘ] − Rf)
frontier|Efficient frontier|What makes a portfolio efficient in mean–variance theory?|No available portfolio improves expected return without increasing risk, or reduces risk at that return.|Efficiency is conditional on inputs and constraints. Estimation error can make the theoretically optimal allocation unstable.|It always earns the most in every outcome.|It contains only safe assets.
cml|Capital market line|How does the capital market line differ from the security market line?|The capital market line (CML) relates efficient portfolios to total volatility; the security market line (SML) relates expected return to beta.|The CML mixes a risk-free asset with the tangency portfolio. Under CAPM, the risky tangency portfolio is the market portfolio.|Both use total volatility for every individual stock.|The CML prices only options.
lln|Law of large numbers|When is insurance pooling most effective?|When many sufficiently independent, measurable losses are pooled.|Common catastrophes remain even in a huge pool. Reinsurance and reserves help handle concentrated losses; they do not make losses disappear.|When every claim happens at once.|When risks are perfectly positively correlated.
var|Value at Risk|Does a 99% one-day VaR of $1 million cap losses at $1 million?|No: it is a loss threshold exceeded about 1% of days under the model.|VaR says little about loss severity beyond the threshold. Expected shortfall examines average tail loss; stress tests examine selected scenarios.|Yes, it is the worst possible loss.|Yes, except for transaction fees.
tails|Fat tails and stress tests|Why can a normal-distribution risk model underestimate danger?|Extreme outcomes can be more frequent and dependence can worsen in crises.|Historical samples may miss rare events. Stress tests complement statistical models but are scenarios, not forecasts with certain probabilities.|Markets never experience unusual events.|Normal models always overstate losses.
utility|Expected utility|Why might investors reject a positive expected-value gamble?|With diminishing marginal utility, losses can hurt more than equal gains help.|Risk preferences concern wealth and obligations. Expected utility is a model; behavioral reference dependence is a distinct explanation.|A positive expected value guarantees a loss.|A positive expected value implies equal utility for everyone.
''')
topic('behavior','03 · Prices & psychology','Efficiency, forecasts, bubbles & narratives',
'''Prices reflect expectations about future cash flows and the rates used to discount them. A good company can be a poor purchase at an excessive price. EMH concerns information and returns relative to a risk benchmark; it does not say that every price is unquestionably correct. Testing efficiency also tests the model of expected returns.\n\nShiller’s excess-volatility work challenges simple constant-discount-rate accounts of prices. Behavioral finance adds reference dependence, overconfidence and social feedback. Even a correct valuation view can be difficult to trade: financing, short-sale limits and uncertain timing constrain arbitrage.''',
'''
news|Expectations versus news|Why might a stock fall after announcing record earnings?|The news may be worse than investors already expected.|Price reactions depend on the surprise and changes to future expectations, not whether the headline sounds positive.|Record earnings always imply a rising price.|The market must have ignored the announcement.
emh|Forms of efficiency|What information does semi-strong market efficiency cover?|All publicly available information.|Weak form covers past trading data; strong form includes private information and is not a realistic general description. Efficiency is relative to an information set.|Only historical prices.|Every private secret without exception.
walk|Random walks|Does efficient pricing require constant expected returns?|No: expected risk premiums can change even if unexpected news is unpredictable.|A strict random walk is stronger than many efficient-market models. Predictability associated with risk does not alone establish free profits.|Yes, all assets must have identical expected returns.|Yes, yesterday’s price must equal tomorrow’s.
joint|Joint-hypothesis problem|Why is poor model performance not a clean test of market inefficiency?|A test of abnormal returns also relies on an asset-pricing model.|Apparent alpha can reflect a missing risk factor or a mispriced asset. Neither interpretation follows automatically.|Because prices cannot be measured.|Because all asset-pricing models are exact laws.
excess|Excess volatility|What does Shiller’s excess-volatility argument challenge?|Simple valuations with constant discount rates that struggle to explain price swings.|Changing discount rates and risk premiums matter. The argument does not prove that every large movement is irrational.|The existence of dividend payments.|Every theory permitting changing expected returns.
prospect|Prospect theory|How do reference points affect decisions in prospect theory?|People evaluate gains and losses relative to a reference point and weight them asymmetrically.|Loss aversion, diminishing sensitivity and nonlinear probability weighting can explain choices beyond standard expected utility. Loss aversion is not identical to ordinary risk aversion.|Only final wealth matters, regardless of framing.|People always take less risk after losses.
anchoring|Anchoring|Why is your purchase price a poor reason by itself to keep a stock?|It can anchor the decision even when future prospects have changed.|The relevant choice compares future outcomes and alternatives from today. Tax consequences can matter, but the past price does not determine economic value.|The purchase price determines future earnings.|Selling below your purchase price is always irrational.
disposition|Disposition effect|What is the disposition effect?|A tendency to sell winners and hold losers too long.|It can reflect reluctance to realize losses. A useful check is whether you would buy the same holding today given its prospects.|A proven strategy to maximize return.|Selling losing assets immediately in every case.
confidence|Overconfidence|How can overconfidence reduce investment performance?|It can lead to excessive trading, concentration and underestimated uncertainty.|Distinguish a persuasive story from calibrated evidence. Costs and taxes can erode returns even when gross predictions have some skill.|It eliminates estimation error.|More trades always create more information.
bubbles|Feedback and bubbles|How can price increases help sustain a speculative boom?|Rising prices attract attention, reinforce stories and loosen borrowing constraints.|Feedback need not follow a universal sequence. Fundamentals may improve too; a compelling technology does not justify every price.|A bubble has a mechanically known end date.|Price increases prove future cash flows rose proportionately.
arbitrage|Limits to arbitrage|Why can an apparently overpriced asset stay expensive?|Trading against it can involve funding risk, borrow costs and uncertain timing.|Being right about value is different from surviving the trade. A short seller can face losses, recalls and margin demands before prices adjust.|Every mispricing disappears instantly.|No one can ever identify valuation risks.
narratives|Narrative economics|Why does Shiller care about stories spreading socially?|Shared narratives can change spending, investment and risk-taking.|This is a mechanism for expectations and feedback, not permission to replace evidence with anecdotes. Narratives can spread faster than fundamentals change.|Stories mechanically determine every market price.|Narratives matter only after a crisis ends.
''')
topic('bonds','04 · Money, bonds & yields','Cash flows first; prices and rates second',
'''A conventional bond promises coupons and principal. Draw the timeline before using a formula. C is the coupon per payment period; F is face value; t indexes a payment; T is the number of remaining periods; y is yield per period. In the final period, pay C + F, not F alone.\n\nYTM is the single discount rate that makes promised cash flows equal the observed price. It is an internal rate of return, not a guaranteed terminal compound return: that interpretation requires coupon reinvestment at the same rate. With a nonflat yield curve, value payments using their own spot discount factors. Credit risk means promised and expected payments differ. Annual, semiannual and continuous conventions must not be mixed.''',
'''
face|Face value and coupon|A bond has a face value of $1,000 and a 5% annual coupon rate, paid once a year. What is its annual coupon payment, and how much principal is repaid at maturity?|The annual coupon payment (C) is $50; the face value (F), or principal repaid at maturity, is $1,000.|Face value is a contractual principal amount, not necessarily the purchase price. The final payment includes both the final coupon and principal.|The annual coupon payment is $1,000; the principal repaid is $50.|The annual coupon payment is $5; the principal repaid is $1,000.|C = F × c / m
time|Payment periods and maturity|In the bond-pricing formula shown below, why does the payment-period index change while the total number of payment periods stays fixed? Use a three-year bond with annual coupons as an example.|The payment-period index (t) runs through 1, 2 and 3; the total number of payment periods until maturity (T) stays fixed at 3.|For semiannual coupons on the same maturity, T is 6 and t indexes half-years. Match the yield and coupon to those periods.|The payment-period index is the coupon rate; total periods means market price.|Both symbols always mean calendar years.|P = Σₜ₌₁ᵀ C/(1+y)ᵗ + F/(1+y)ᵀ
market|Who sets market yield?|Does an issuer centrally dictate the yield accepted by investors?|No: offered terms meet investor demand, and traded prices imply yields.|An issuer and underwriters can propose a coupon and issue price. Bookbuilding tests demand; secondary transactions occur between investors. Comparable bond yields are observations, not a universal imposed rate.|Yes, investors must accept the announced yield.|Yes, a fixed coupon prevents price changes.
ytminverse|YTM as an implied rate|How do you find the yield to maturity (YTM) for a traded bond?|Solve for the rate that discounts its promised cash flows to its current price.|Use the observed price as input; valuation exercises can reverse the calculation and use a required yield to find a price. A complex bond can require yield-to-call or other measures.|Divide its face value by its price.|Use the coupon rate regardless of price.
current|Current yield versus YTM|How is current yield different from YTM?|Current yield is annual coupon divided by price; YTM also incorporates repayment timing and price-to-par change.|Neither is automatically an expected return after default losses. A discount bond can have current yield below YTM.|They are always equal.|Current yield includes every possible future capital gain.|Current yield = annual coupon / market price
reinvest|Reinvestment risk|Does holding a coupon bond to maturity guarantee terminal wealth compounding at YTM?|No: that terminal-wealth interpretation requires reinvesting coupons at YTM.|The cash-flow IRR remains YTM if promised payments arrive. What coupons earn after receipt is a separate decision and risk. Zero-coupon bonds have no interim coupon reinvestment.|Yes, regardless of what you do with coupons.|No, because the face value changes daily.
price|Bond prices and yields|What usually happens to a fixed-rate bond price when its required yield rises?|Its price falls.|The contract pays the same amounts but those amounts are discounted more heavily. This relationship assumes unchanged contractual cash flows.|Its coupon automatically rises.|Its price must remain at face value.
spot|Spot rates versus YTM|Why might each bond payment need a different discount rate?|Different maturities can have different spot rates.|YTM compresses a whole cash-flow schedule into one rate. Arbitrage-consistent pricing uses maturity-specific discount factors, then can report the implied YTM.|All payments always face one identical market spot rate.|Coupons are not discounted.|P = Σ CFₜ × DF(t)
forward|Forward interest rates|Is an interest rate implied by today’s yield curve a certain future spot rate?|No: it is a no-arbitrage forward rate and may include risk-premium effects.|The expectations hypothesis adds assumptions about premiums. With annual compounding, a two-year investment and a rolled one-year investment define the one-year forward rate.|Yes, it is a perfect forecast.|Yes, it is fixed by the issuer for everyone.|(1+s₂)² = (1+s₁)(1+f₁,₂)
duration|Modified duration|What does modified duration of 8 imply for a 1 percentage-point yield rise?|Approximately an 8% price decline for a small parallel yield change.|Use Δy = 0.01. Macaulay duration is a cash-flow-weighted time measure; modified duration converts that to sensitivity. Convexity improves the approximation.|Approximately an 8% price increase.|Approximately a 0.08% price decline.|ΔP/P ≈ −Dmod Δy; Dmod = DMac/(1+y) for annual compounding
convexity|Convexity and embedded options|Why can duration alone be inaccurate for large yield moves?|The price–yield relation is curved, and options can change expected cash flows.|A standard option-free bond has positive convexity. Callable bonds and mortgage securities can show negative convexity as borrowers refinance when rates fall.|All bond prices are linear in yields.|Duration measures default probability exactly.|ΔP/P ≈ −Dmod Δy + ½ convexity (Δy)²
curve|Yield curves|Does an inverted yield curve guarantee a recession?|No: it is a historical warning indicator, not a deterministic rule.|Expected policy rates, term premiums, liquidity and demand can shape the curve. Compare comparable debt, currencies and yield conventions.|Yes, on a known date.|No, inversions never contain information.
credit|Credit spreads|Is a corporate bond’s spread pure expected default loss?|No: it can include default compensation, risk premiums and liquidity effects.|Compare similar duration, currency and optionality. A high promised yield can coexist with a modest or negative expected return.|Yes, with no risk premium.|No, the spread is simply its coupon.
clean|Clean and dirty prices|Why can the cash paid for a bond exceed its quoted clean price?|The settlement amount adds accrued coupon interest.|The dirty price includes accrued interest since the last coupon. Accrual conventions and dates matter; do not confuse it with a hidden return.|The broker changes its face value.|The coupon has become a stock dividend.
real|Nominal versus real returns|What does inflation do to a nominal investment return?|It reduces the purchasing-power return.|Match nominal cash flows with nominal discount rates, and real cash flows with real rates. Inflation-indexed bonds address specified index risk, not every household’s exact costs.|It always raises the real return.|It changes nothing if principal is repaid.|1 + real return = (1 + nominal return)/(1 + inflation)
annuity|Annuities and perpetuities|How does a level annuity differ from a perpetuity?|An annuity pays for a finite number of periods; a perpetuity continues indefinitely.|A consol is a perpetual bond. These formulas assume level end-of-period payments and a positive constant discount rate.|Both stop at the same known date.|A perpetuity has no present value.|PVannuity = C[1 − (1+r)^−T]/r; PVperpetuity = C/r
''')
topic('equity','05 · Stocks & corporations','Valuation, ownership & capital structure',
'''Common equity is a residual claim: creditors get paid before shareholders. Value depends on future distributable cash flows and required returns. The Gordon growth model is a useful special case, but small changes in r − g can produce large valuation changes. A mature steady-growth company is very different from a young firm with uncertain reinvestment needs.\n\nKeep enterprise value and equity value separate. Discount cash flow to all capital providers at WACC, then reconcile debt, cash and other claims. Financing and payout policy move claims around; dividends and buybacks do not create free wealth on their own. CAPE adds a long-horizon valuation perspective without supplying a reliable short-term trading signal.''',
'''
residual|Residual ownership|Who normally has priority over common shareholders in liquidation?|Creditors and other senior claims.|A stockholder owns a residual interest, not a guaranteed claim to return of the original purchase price. Limited liability does not prevent a total equity loss.|Common shareholders always get paid first.|Every stakeholder receives the same amount.
preferred|Common and preferred shares|What usually distinguishes preferred shares from common shares?|Preferred shares usually have specified dividend and liquidation priority, with different voting rights.|Terms vary. Preferred dividends may be suspendable and sometimes cumulative; preferred equity is not automatically a safe bond.|Preferred shares guarantee unlimited voting power.|Common shares have priority over every preferred share.
gordon|Gordon growth valuation|Under what assumptions is the Gordon growth valuation formula shown below meaningful?|When dividends grow perpetually at a constant rate below the required return.|D₁ is next period’s dividend, r is the required equity return, g is perpetual growth. A growth rate at or above r makes this infinite-horizon formula invalid.|When g is greater than r forever.|When D₁ means the stock’s market price.|P₀ = D₁/(r − g), with r > g
wacc|Enterprise and equity value|If you discount free cash flow to the firm at the weighted average cost of capital (WACC), which value do you estimate?|Enterprise value, before reconciling debt and other claims to get equity value.|Subtract debt and senior claims, add nonoperating cash where appropriate. Equity cash flows instead use the cost of equity. Consistent treatment avoids double counting.|Only the value of the ordinary shares.|Only the company’s bank deposits.|WACC = E/(D+E) × Re + D/(D+E) × Rd × (1−tax rate)
terminal|Terminal value|Why do small terminal-growth changes have large DCF effects?|Much of estimated value may lie in the distant cash flows, and r − g can be small.|A perpetually growing terminal value must fit a sustainable long-run economy and a mature cash-flow base. Sensitivity analysis is more honest than a single precise number.|Terminal value is independent of discount rates.|Terminal value means current cash in the bank.
pe|Price-to-earnings ratios|Does a low P/E automatically mean a bargain?|No: it can reflect weak growth, unusual earnings or high risk.|Use comparable accounting and earnings periods. Negative or temporarily inflated earnings make the ratio unhelpful. Valuation requires a story about future cash flows and uncertainty.|Yes, low P/E guarantees excess returns.|Yes, P/E is a contractual payout.
cape|CAPE|What does CAPE smooth, and what is its main use?|Ten years of real earnings, to inform long-horizon valuation comparisons.|It can help frame expected returns, but cannot reliably time next month’s market. Accounting, sector composition, payout policy and discount rates affect comparisons.|It predicts the exact date of a crash.|It uses only next quarter’s nominal earnings.|CAPE = price index / average inflation-adjusted earnings over 10 years
marketcap|Market capitalization|How do you compute a company’s market capitalization?|Share price times shares outstanding.|This is an equity value, not enterprise value or the amount the company raised. Price changes can alter market cap without cash entering the firm.|Total revenue plus dividends.|Share price times total debt.
dilution|Dilution|Does issuing more shares necessarily destroy existing shareholders’ value?|No: ownership percentage falls, but new capital or acquired value may offset it.|If shares are issued cheaply relative to value, existing owners may lose. Distinguish percentage dilution from economic dilution and earnings-per-share effects.|Yes, new investment never adds value.|No, issuance cannot change existing owners’ wealth.
buyback|Dividends and buybacks|Is a share repurchase automatically a wealth-creating event?|No: it distributes cash and changes shares outstanding; price and financing matter.|An ex-dividend price tends to adjust for the payout, all else equal. Buybacks can raise EPS mechanically without improving business profits.|Yes, EPS rises so total economic value must rise.|Yes, shareholders get free money with no asset reduction.
npv|Net present value|When does a project add value under an NPV rule?|When discounted incremental cash flows exceed the required initial investment.|Use opportunity costs and an appropriate risk-adjusted rate. Exclude sunk costs, include working capital and relevant taxes. A positive accounting profit is not sufficient.|Whenever gross revenue is positive.|Whenever management likes the narrative.|NPV = −initial investment + Σ incremental CFₜ/(1+r)ᵗ
capitalstructure|Capital structure|Why doesn’t leverage create value for free?|It reallocates risk and can introduce taxes, distress costs and incentive effects.|Modigliani–Miller irrelevance is a benchmark under restrictive assumptions. Real funding choices matter because those assumptions fail.|Debt has no repayment obligation.|Borrowing eliminates operating risk.
''')
topic('housing','06 · Housing & mortgages','Amortization, prepayment & securitization',
'''A home provides housing services as well as uncertain investment returns. Compare total costs: financing, taxes, maintenance, insurance, vacancy, transaction costs and the alternative use of capital. A cap rate uses property-level net operating income before debt service; it is not the investor’s complete return.\n\nA mortgage payment combines interest and principal. Early payments on a level-payment loan are mostly interest because the outstanding balance is larger. Fixed-rate borrowers can often refinance if rates fall, giving them an option at the lender’s expense. Securitization pools loans and reallocates payments and losses; pooling correlated collateral does not make its common risk disappear.''',
'''
caprate|Capitalization rate|Does a property’s cap rate equal its leveraged total return?|No: cap rate is NOI divided by property value, before financing and appreciation.|NOI normally subtracts operating expenses but excludes debt service and income taxes; treatment of reserves and major capital expenses needs checking. Vacancy and costs can change it.|Yes, regardless of debt and expenses.|Yes, cap rate includes all future appreciation.|Cap rate = net operating income / property value
rentbuy|Rent versus buy|Why is comparing rent only with a mortgage payment incomplete?|Ownership also has maintenance, taxes, insurance, transaction costs and capital opportunity costs.|Principal repayment builds equity but ties up cash. Consider housing services, flexibility, horizon and uncertain resale value; price appreciation is not guaranteed.|Because rent always builds equity.|Because homes never require maintenance.
amortization|Mortgage amortization|Why does the principal portion of a fixed monthly payment rise over time?|Interest is charged on a shrinking outstanding balance, leaving more of the payment for principal.|The payment may be constant while its composition changes. Taxes and insurance may be separate. With zero interest, payment is simply principal divided by periods.|The contractual interest rate must fall each month.|Principal is never repaid before maturity.|Payment = L × i/[1 − (1+i)^−n]; i = monthly rate, n = payments
ltv|Loan-to-value|Why does a high LTV leave little protection against falling home prices?|A small equity cushion can be wiped out by a modest asset decline.|LTV is loan balance divided by property value. Negative equity and inability to refinance can interact, though legal recourse and borrower incentives differ across markets.|A higher LTV means more homeowner equity.|Home prices cannot fall below loan balances.
refinance|Prepayment and refinancing|Who benefits from a borrower’s right to refinance a fixed-rate mortgage when rates fall?|The borrower; the lender may receive principal back when reinvestment yields are lower.|Prepayment also reflects moves and other events. Mortgage-backed investors face prepayment and extension risk, not merely credit risk.|Only the lender, who keeps the old coupons forever.|Nobody, because fixed rates cannot be replaced.
arm|Adjustable-rate mortgages|What risk does an adjustable-rate borrower retain?|Future payments can rise when the reset reference rate rises.|Margins, caps, reset dates and amortization terms matter. A low initial rate does not describe all future payments; a fixed rate shifts more rate risk to the lender.|Payments must fall every year.|The interest rate can never change after issuance.
mbs|Mortgage-backed securities|What generates the cash flows of a mortgage-backed security?|Payments from a pool of underlying mortgage loans.|Pool structure, servicing, guarantees and prepayment determine the allocation. A guarantee against credit loss does not eliminate interest-rate or prepayment exposure.|Unrelated stock dividends.|A risk-free government payout in every MBS.
tranches|Tranches and correlation|Why can a senior tranche lose money even if it has first payment priority?|Underlying losses can become large enough to exhaust subordinated protection.|Priority changes who loses first. Highly correlated defaults can overwhelm a design that looked safe using independent-loss assumptions.|Senior status rules out every loss.|Tranching eliminates underlying defaults.
cds|Credit default swaps|What does a CDS generally transfer?|Defined credit-event risk through premium and protection payments.|It is not identical to owning a bond or receiving fire insurance. Reference obligations, settlement rules, counterparty risk and basis risk matter.|All interest-rate risk automatically.|A guaranteed ownership share in the borrower.
reits|REITs and property exposure|Does a listed REIT trade like direct property ownership?|No: it is a tradable equity claim on a real-estate business with its own leverage and costs.|REITs can improve access and liquidity, but still carry property, financing and market risks. Tax and distribution rules depend on jurisdiction.|Yes, with identical liquidity and taxes everywhere.|No, a REIT contains no real-estate exposure.
''')
topic('derivatives','07 · Derivatives & hedging','Replication, payoff diagrams & no-arbitrage',
'''A derivative’s value comes from its contractual payoff, the underlying exposures and the terms of settlement. Hedging exchanges one pattern of outcomes for another; it does not promise higher profits. A futures hedge may work economically yet demand cash margin before the underlying business receives its offsetting cash.\n\nNo-arbitrage connects portfolios with identical future payoffs. Replication can price a claim without knowing real-world probabilities, if the modeled states and trading opportunities are sufficient. Distinguish payoff from profit, and option rights from exercise obligations. Models assume conditions that real markets only approximate; jumps, costs, uncertain volatility and collateral terms limit hedging.''',
'''
forward|Forward contracts|What is a long forward’s expiration payoff?|The underlying settlement price minus the agreed delivery price.|An at-market forward usually starts with approximately zero value; its delivery price differs from its later market value. The buyer is obligated, unlike an option holder.|The maximum of the price difference and zero.|Always the full underlying price.|Long forward payoff = S_T − K
futures|Futures and forwards|What operational feature distinguishes standard futures from typical forwards?|Exchange standardization and daily marking to market through clearing.|OTC forwards can also have collateral agreements. Clearing concentrates and manages counterparty risk; it does not remove margin, liquidity or clearinghouse risk.|Futures never need collateral.|Forwards always give a right without obligation.
carry|Cost-of-carry pricing|When is the forward-pricing formula shown below an appropriate no-arbitrage benchmark?|For a non-income-producing asset with financing at r and no other carry benefits or costs.|Dividend yield changes equity forwards to S₀e^((r−q)T). Commodities may add storage costs and convenience yield; FX involves both currencies’ interest rates.|For every commodity regardless of storage.|Only when future prices are certain.|F₀ = S₀e^(rT); with dividend yield q: S₀e^((r−q)T)
basis|Basis risk|Why might a hedge fail to offset the business exposure exactly?|The hedge instrument and actual exposure can move differently.|Different commodities, locations, timing or grades create basis risk. A hedge can reduce overall risk without being perfect.|Every hedge guarantees exact cancellation.|Because hedge prices never change.
call|Call payoff and profit|A call finishes in the money. Is it necessarily profitable for its buyer?|No: the payoff may be smaller than the premium and costs.|At expiration payoff is max(S_T−K,0); simple profit subtracts premium. An unexercised worthless option costs the buyer its premium.|Yes, in the money means positive net profit.|No, a call can never have positive payoff.|Call payoff = max(S_T − K,0); simple profit = payoff − premium
put|Buying a put|Must you own a stock to buy a put and profit from its decline?|No: the option can be bought independently.|You can normally sell the option to realize value. Exercise and physical settlement can create other positions or requirements; owning a put alone does not automatically make you short.|Yes, ownership is always required.|Buying a put immediately sells the stock short.|Put payoff = max(K − S_T,0)
exercise|European and American options|How does an American-style option differ from a European-style option?|It permits exercise before expiration under its terms.|The names describe exercise rights, not trading geography. Early exercise can matter for dividends, deep in-the-money puts and interest; selling can preserve time value.|It trades only in America.|It never expires.
parity|Put–call parity|What is the intuition behind European put–call parity?|Two portfolios with identical terminal payoffs must have the same value absent arbitrage.|For no dividends and matching strike and maturity: call plus discounted strike cash matches stock plus put. Dividends, early exercise and financing constraints alter the relation.|Calls and puts must always have equal prices.|Any option is worth the underlying price.|C + K e^(−rT) = P + S₀
binomial|Binomial replication|Why can a two-state option be priced without real-world up/down probabilities?|A stock-and-cash portfolio can replicate its payoffs in both states.|The hedge ratio is the change in option payoff divided by the change in stock price. The risk-free gross return must lie between the up and down multipliers for no arbitrage.|Because every up move has probability one-half.|Because option sellers can choose future stock prices.|Δ = (Cᵤ − C_d)/(Sᵤ − S_d)
riskneutral|Risk-neutral probabilities|Are risk-neutral probabilities investors’ actual forecasts?|No: they are pricing weights consistent with no-arbitrage and discounting at the risk-free rate.|In a binomial model, q = [(1+r)−d]/(u−d). Real-world expected returns include risk compensation; pricing weights serve a different purpose.|Yes, they are measured survey probabilities.|Yes, risk-neutral investors guarantee no losses.
bs|Black–Scholes–Merton|Why do observed volatility smiles challenge basic Black–Scholes assumptions?|A single constant volatility does not fit option prices across strikes and maturities.|The model assumes a particular continuous price process and frictionless dynamic hedging. Implied volatility is the model input matching a price, not a certain future realized volatility.|The model includes every possible jump exactly.|The model requires options to be free.|C = S₀N(d₁) − K e^(−rT)N(d₂); d₁ = [ln(S₀/K)+(r+σ²/2)T]/(σ√T); d₂ = d₁−σ√T
vol|Implied and realized volatility|What is implied volatility?|The volatility input that makes an option-pricing model match the observed option price.|It can include risk premiums and supply-demand effects. Realized volatility measures actual price dispersion over a chosen period; they need not agree.|A guaranteed forecast of future volatility.|The strike price divided by spot price.
greeks|Option Greeks|What risks do delta, gamma, theta, vega and rho describe?|Sensitivity to price, delta changes, time, volatility and interest rates respectively.|Units and conventions matter. Gamma measures curvature; theta usually measures change as calendar time passes; vega is often quoted per volatility point.|Five different default probabilities.|Only the chance the option expires worthless.
deltahedge|Delta hedging|What does delta hedging aim to offset?|First-order exposure to changes in the underlying price.|Delta changes, so hedging must be rebalanced. Gamma, jumps, volatility changes, funding and discrete trading leave risks. A short call often requires buying underlying shares.|Every conceivable risk.|A guaranteed positive profit.
short|Short selling|How do short-stock losses differ from long-put losses?|Short-stock losses can be unlimited; a long put generally risks its premium.|Stock borrowing also has fees, recalls and margin demands. A put has expiry and time value, so timing matters even if the long-term direction is right.|Both always lose only a fixed premium.|A short stock has a fixed expiration date in all cases.
embedded|Embedded options|Where can an option appear outside exchange-traded contracts?|In refinancing rights, callable debt, guarantees and investment decisions.|A firm’s ability to delay or abandon a project resembles a real option. Recognizing optionality improves cash-flow and risk analysis.|Only in listed equity calls.|Only in contracts labeled option.
''')
topic('banking','08 · Banks & central banks','Balance sheets, money creation & resilience',
'''A bank’s assets are financed by liabilities and equity. Deposits are liabilities to the bank. Equity absorbs losses; reserves provide settlement liquidity. Neither is interchangeable with the other. Transforming short-term funding into long-lived loans supports enterprise but creates run risk.\n\nBank lending commonly creates a deposit, but lending remains constrained by capital, funding, liquidity, risk and demand. Central banks influence rates and settlement conditions. QE exchanges assets and changes balance sheets; it is not automatically household spending. Regulation combines capital quality, risk-weighted requirements, leverage limits, liquidity rules and supervision. Basel standards require domestic implementation and are not one globally identical rule.''',
'''
balance|Bank balance sheets|On a bank’s balance sheet, are customer deposits assets or liabilities?|Liabilities: money the bank owes its customers.|Loans and securities are assets. Equity is assets minus liabilities and absorbs losses. Depositor withdrawals reduce both deposits and the assets used to pay them.|Assets because customers own them.|Equity because deposits absorb every loss first.
capital|Capital versus cash|Is regulatory capital a pot of cash kept in a vault?|No: it consists of qualifying loss-absorbing funding claims, especially equity.|Cash and reserves are assets used for payments. A bank can have high capital and little immediate liquidity, or plenty of cash and inadequate solvency.|Yes, capital and reserves are identical.|Yes, capital is the interest rate charged on loans.
liquid|Liquidity versus solvency|How can a solvent bank fail during a run?|It may be unable to raise cash fast enough without destructive asset sales.|Solvency concerns economic asset value versus liabilities. Liquidity concerns meeting payments now. Forced sales can turn a liquidity problem into a solvency problem.|Its loans vanish automatically.|A solvent bank always has all deposits in cash.
creation|Loan-created deposits|What often happens when a commercial bank makes a new loan?|It records a loan asset and a corresponding customer deposit liability.|This expands deposits, not the bank’s equity. Lending and payments still require funding, capital and liquidity management. Loan principal repayment generally reverses the creation.|It must first find an identical existing deposit to lend.|It creates new equity with no liability.
reserves|Reserves and the multiplier|Why is a fixed textbook money multiplier an incomplete banking model?|Banks’ lending decisions face many constraints beyond reserve ratios.|Reserves settle payments among banks; households hold deposits, not central-bank reserves. More reserves do not mechanically force banks to grant more credit.|Because banks face no constraints.|Because deposits and reserves are the same claim.
policy|Monetary-policy transmission|How can a higher policy rate slow demand?|It influences financing costs, asset prices, exchange rates and expectations.|Transmission has delays and differs by borrower, debt maturity and financial system. Central banks influence conditions but do not directly set every bond yield.|It instantly fixes every consumer price.|It mandates the same interest rate on every loan.
qe|Quantitative easing|Does QE necessarily mean handing new cash directly to households?|No: it involves central-bank asset purchases and corresponding balance-sheet changes.|Reserve balances rise. Effects depend on who sells, portfolio responses and credit conditions; reserve creation alone does not imply a proportional spending increase.|Yes, it is always a household grant.|Yes, it eliminates all public debt.
qt|Quantitative tightening|How can QT shrink a central bank’s securities portfolio?|By letting assets mature without full reinvestment or selling assets.|Its effects depend on implementation, liquidity conditions and other balance-sheet movements. QT is different from raising the policy rate, though both can tighten conditions.|By increasing every bond’s coupon.|By deleting borrowers’ contractual debts.
lender|Lender of last resort|What problem is emergency central-bank lending designed to address?|Acute funding and liquidity stress, subject to the facility’s terms.|Collateral, eligibility and pricing matter. Lending cannot by itself restore an institution whose economic losses exceed its equity; fiscal and resolution choices may be needed.|It guarantees every institution is solvent.|It replaces all supervision permanently.
rwa|Risk-weighted capital|How do risk-weighted assets (RWA) differ from total assets?|Exposures are adjusted by regulatory risk weights rather than simply added at face value.|A leverage ratio adds a differently defined non-risk-weighted backstop. Buffers, capital quality and off-balance-sheet exposures matter; an 8% example is not every bank’s complete requirement.|RWA are the total interest paid by borrowers.|RWA are cash held by depositors.|Capital ratio = qualifying regulatory capital / RWA
liquidityrules|Liquidity regulation|What do the LCR and NSFR broadly address?|Short-term liquidity under stress and stability of funding over a longer horizon.|The liquidity coverage ratio uses high-quality liquid assets and stressed outflows; the net stable funding ratio addresses funding structure. They complement capital requirements.|The same thing as stock-market beta.|Only the tax rate on deposits.
insurance|Deposit insurance|Why can deposit insurance reduce bank runs but create incentives to take risk?|It reassures covered depositors while potentially weakening their monitoring.|Coverage depends on jurisdiction, institution, ownership category and limits. Supervision and loss-absorbing capital help contain moral hazard; not every account or product is covered.|It insures every financial asset worldwide.|It eliminates the need for capital.
''')
topic('institutions','09 · Institutions & trading','Funds, intermediaries & market plumbing',
'''Separate roles: brokers act for clients, dealers trade on their own account, market makers provide quotes, and investment banks arrange issuance and corporate transactions. Some groups perform several roles, making conflicts and governance relevant. A rating is an opinion about credit, not insurance.\n\nFunds make diversified holdings accessible, but fees, underlying exposures and redemption terms still matter. ETFs use a creation/redemption mechanism that usually links trading price and NAV, without guaranteeing identical prices in stressed markets. Execution quality is broader than a zero commission: spreads, slippage, routing and conflicts affect what investors receive.''',
'''
investmentbank|Commercial and investment banking|What is a typical investment-banking function?|Underwriting securities, advising on M&A and facilitating capital-market transactions.|Commercial banks focus on deposits, payments and credit. Large groups can combine businesses through different legal entities and regulation.|Only accepting household cash deposits.|Guaranteeing stock-market returns.
underwriting|Underwriting and IPOs|What risk does firm-commitment underwriting put on the underwriter?|The distribution risk of buying securities from the issuer and reselling them under the agreement.|Best-efforts arrangements differ. Bookbuilding gathers investor demand; a first-day price jump does not mean pricing was obvious beforehand.|No risk because every IPO must rise.|All long-term business risk forever.
ratings|Credit ratings|Does an investment-grade rating guarantee repayment?|No: it is a credit opinion that can change or be wrong.|Ratings are not a full liquidity or interest-rate-risk measure. Issuer-paid models and concentration can create conflicts; investors still need analysis.|Yes, ratings agencies reimburse losses.|Yes, a rating fixes the bond price at par.
roles|Broker, dealer and market maker|What is the difference between a broker and a dealer?|A broker acts for clients; a dealer trades for its own account.|A market maker offers buying and selling quotes and manages inventory. A firm can have multiple roles, making disclosure and conflict management important.|A broker must own every security it sells.|A dealer never holds inventory.
spread|Bid–ask spreads|What is the bid–ask spread?|The quoted ask price minus the quoted bid price.|A buyer commonly pays ask and a seller receives bid. Inventory risk, adverse selection and processing costs help explain the spread.|The dividend divided by price.|The difference between face value and coupon.
orders|Market and limit orders|Which order prioritizes a price constraint over certainty of immediate execution?|A limit order.|A market order prioritizes execution but its price can move, particularly in thin markets. A limit order may never execute. Stop orders have separate trigger and execution risks.|A market order always guarantees a chosen price.|A limit order guarantees immediate execution.
pfof|Payment for order flow|What conflict can payment for order flow create?|A broker’s routing payments may conflict with obtaining the best execution for customers.|Zero stated commission does not prove zero trading cost. Assess execution prices, spreads and routing quality; rules differ across jurisdictions.|It guarantees the best available price in every trade.|It means the broker buys the stock for free.
hft|High-frequency trading|Can fast algorithmic trading both improve and weaken liquidity?|Yes: it can tighten spreads in normal conditions yet liquidity may retreat under stress.|Strategies differ. Speed itself is not a single business model; adverse selection, infrastructure concentration and order behavior affect outcomes.|No, speed always guarantees stability.|No, all fast traders use identical strategies.
funds|Mutual funds and ETFs|Does ETF mean passive index fund?|No: an ETF can be active or passive.|ETF describes a wrapper with intraday exchange trading. Open-end mutual fund transactions typically occur at a calculated NAV. Strategies and costs vary.|Yes, every ETF tracks a broad stock index.|Yes, ETFs cannot hold bonds.
nav|ETF price versus NAV|Why is an ETF’s exchange price usually close to its net asset value?|Authorized participants can create or redeem units, supporting arbitrage.|Underlying liquidity and transaction costs can limit the mechanism. A discount during stress can also reflect stale underlying marks, not necessarily free profit.|The issuer legally fixes every exchange trade price.|ETF shares cannot trade below NAV.
fees|Fees and compounding|Why do small annual fees matter over decades?|They reduce the return that compounds each year.|Compare total costs, taxes and tracking difference rather than headline fees alone. An inexpensive concentrated fund is still concentrated.|Because fees compound positively for the investor.|Because fees affect only the first year.
endowment|Endowment investing|Why can’t an individual simply copy a major endowment’s allocation?|Access, governance, liquidity needs and ability to manage illiquid investments differ.|Swensen’s broader lessons include diversification, long horizons, costs and matching a strategy to obligations. Manager selection is difficult and not all alternatives improve returns.|Endowments face no risk.|Illiquid investments always deliver superior returns.
''')
topic('crises','10 · Leverage & crises','Funding runs, contagion & resolution',
'''Leverage magnifies gains and losses relative to the investor’s equity. Margin and collateral requirements can force a sale before an investment recovers. During a boom, stronger collateral supports more borrowing; during a decline, the mechanism reverses. Funding maturity and the ability to absorb losses matter as much as asset labels.\n\nThe 2008 crisis combined housing losses, fragile funding, high leverage and opaque interconnections. The 2023 bank failures showed that low-credit-risk securities can still have large duration losses. System resilience requires looking across banks and nonbanks, shared holdings, derivatives and infrastructure. Regulation, supervision, incentives and credible resolution all contribute.''',
'''
leverage|Leverage|Why does a 10% asset decline cause a 50% equity loss when leverage is five times?|Losses are borne by an equity cushion equal to one-fifth of the assets.|For fixed debt and ignoring financing costs, equity return equals asset return times assets/equity. A sufficiently large decline can wipe out the cushion.|Borrowed money absorbs all losses first.|Leverage changes only gains.|Leverage = assets/equity; equity = assets − debt
margin|Margin calls|Why can a profitable long-run idea still fail for a leveraged investor?|Collateral demands may force liquidation before the expected recovery.|Funding liquidity and market liquidity interact. Initial margin and variation margin are different; price moves can cause cash needs without implying an equivalent final economic loss.|Lenders must wait indefinitely.|Margin calls occur only after maturity.
cycle|Leverage cycles|How can falling collateral values deepen a downturn?|They tighten borrowing capacity, triggering sales that depress prices further.|Haircuts can rise at the same time prices fall. A feedback loop can turn modest shocks into large dislocations even without new fundamental news.|Lower collateral values always increase borrowing limits.|Every forced sale raises asset prices.
2008|The 2008 crisis|Why is blaming only one failed bank an incomplete explanation of 2008?|Housing losses interacted with leverage, weak underwriting, securitization and fragile funding across institutions.|Lehman’s failure intensified stress, but vulnerabilities preceded it. Complexity, ratings and correlated mortgage exposures amplified losses and uncertainty.|One isolated event created every preceding mortgage loss.|All government bonds defaulted simultaneously.
svb|SVB and duration risk|What did SVB demonstrate about government-backed securities held by a bank?|Low credit risk does not eliminate duration losses or funding-run vulnerability.|Long-duration asset losses combined with concentrated uninsured deposits and weak risk management. Supervisory shortcomings also mattered.|Government backing guarantees a stable market price.|A bank cannot fail if its assets include Treasuries.
contagion|Contagion channels|How can distress spread without a direct loan between two banks?|Shared asset holdings, funding markets and confidence can transmit shocks.|Direct exposures are only one channel. Fire sales can mark down others’ assets; common lenders may withdraw funding from several institutions.|It cannot spread without a direct loan.|Only depositors with identical account numbers transmit stress.
shadow|Nonbank intermediation|Why does moving lending outside banks not eliminate systemic risk?|Leverage, liquidity mismatch and interconnectedness can persist in different structures.|Funds and other intermediaries can support credit while creating vulnerabilities in redemption, collateral and funding. Different rules can shift where risk accumulates.|Only regulated banks can experience runs.|Nonbank loans have no default risk.
resolution|Resolution and bail-ins|Why have credible failure-resolution plans?|To manage a failing institution while limiting disruption and allocating losses under legal rules.|Shareholder and creditor losses, continuity of critical functions and funding support require preparation. Bail-in and bail-out mean different loss allocations; legal tools vary.|To ensure no investor can ever lose.|To eliminate the need to identify liabilities.
micro_macro|Microprudential and macroprudential policy|How do macroprudential goals differ from institution-by-institution supervision?|They focus on system-wide feedback, shared exposures and resilience.|Making every firm individually cautious can still cause correlated selling. Buffers, leverage constraints and systemic oversight address interactions.|They only regulate the smallest local lender.|They disregard financial stability entirely.
''')
topic('society','11 · Finance & society','Public debt, pensions, ethics & access',
'''Government borrowing can finance productive assets and stabilize emergencies, but sustainability is about future fiscal capacity and financing conditions. Growth relative to interest costs helps; it does not remove the need for taxes, credible institutions and manageable refinancing. Municipal issuers have different revenues and legal constraints from sovereigns.\n\nInsurance, pensions, nonprofits and cooperatives show that finance serves many objectives. Retirement systems allocate longevity, inflation and investment risks differently. Financial advice and governance should consider client obligations, conflicts and access. A useful ethical question is whether the contract helps people manage real risks and undertake worthwhile projects.''',
'''
debtdynamics|Public debt dynamics|What broad forces move a government’s debt-to-GDP ratio?|Interest relative to GDP growth, the primary balance and valuation or other adjustments.|Use both r and g in consistent nominal or real terms. With a primary deficit p, an approximate formula is Δd ≈ (r−g)d + p; the exact simple ratio uses (r−g)/(1+g) times prior debt.|Only the size of this year’s tax revenue.|Only whether the government prints banknotes.|Δd ≈ (r−g)d + p; exact simple form: dₜ = [(1+r)/(1+g)]dₜ₋₁ + pₜ
currency|Sovereign debt and currency|Does borrowing in its own currency make a government risk-free in every sense?|No: inflation, currency depreciation, institutions and political constraints still matter.|Monetary sovereignty differs from borrowing in foreign currency or sharing a currency. Capacity to create nominal money is not a guarantee of real purchasing power.|Yes, real returns are guaranteed.|Yes, growth and taxation no longer matter.
municipal|Municipal finance|How do general-obligation and revenue bonds differ?|They rely on different repayment sources: broader issuer backing versus specified revenues.|Legal pledges and tax treatment vary. A project revenue bond depends on its defined revenue stream; neither label alone assures safety.|Both are claims on the same corporate stock.|Revenue bonds always have a national government guarantee.
social|Social insurance|Why might governments supply unemployment or old-age insurance?|Broad coverage and redistribution can address risks private markets struggle to pool affordably.|Public systems still face incentive, funding and demographic tradeoffs. Social insurance is not identical to private actuarially priced coverage.|Governments can eliminate all risk at no cost.|Private insurance is impossible for every risk.
pensions|Defined benefit and defined contribution pensions|Who primarily bears investment risk in a defined contribution plan?|The participant, whose retirement resources depend on contributions and investment results.|Defined benefit plans promise a benefit formula and put more funding risk on the sponsor, subject to solvency and legal protections. Longevity and inflation risk also matter.|The employer guarantees the same payout in all outcomes.|The exchange guarantees all returns.
longevity|Annuities and longevity risk|What risk can a lifetime annuity pool?|The risk of outliving one’s assets.|The insurer pools uncertain lifetimes. Inflation, insurer solvency, liquidity loss and contract terms remain relevant. A fixed nominal annuity is not a real spending guarantee.|The risk that every stock rises too fast.|Every household’s exact future living expenses.
nonprofit|Nonprofits and cooperatives|What distinguishes a nonprofit from a cooperative?|A nonprofit restricts profit distribution; a cooperative is owned and governed by members.|Both can earn operating surpluses. Ownership and mission change governance, but neither form automatically solves agency problems.|Neither can receive revenue.|Both must distribute profits to outside shareholders.
advice|Advice and conflicts|Why ask how an adviser is compensated?|Compensation can influence product recommendations and create conflicts.|Professional standards, disclosures and client obligations matter. A prudent decision fits risk capacity, liabilities and horizon; legal duties differ across arrangements and jurisdictions.|Compensation never affects incentives.|High fees guarantee better advice.
democratization|Democratization of finance|What does broader financial access ideally accomplish?|It helps more people finance productive activity and protect important risks.|Access is valuable with understandable terms and suitable protection. Easy access to leveraged speculation alone does not establish improved welfare.|It guarantees equal returns for everyone.|It means every citizen should become a day trader.
''')
topic('modern','12 · Finance today','2026 context, digital assets, AI & private credit',
'''Use durable economic questions to evaluate new products: what is the claim, who owes the money, how is it funded, how can it be redeemed, and what happens under stress? Private credit, tokenization and AI do not erase old problems involving valuation, liquidity, incentives or uncertainty.\n\nDated facts below were checked on October 11, 2026. U.S. T+1 settlement dates from May 2024. U.S. agencies released a capital proposal package in March 2026; separately, September 2026 final stress-test rules introduce annual public input and two-year buffer averaging starting in 2028. A final rule’s announcement is not the same as all provisions already taking effect. This app does not automatically refresh regulations.''',
'''
tplus|T+1 settlement|What does T+1 mean for most covered U.S. securities trades?|Standard settlement one business day after the trade date.|The U.S. transition took effect May 28, 2024. Settlement transfers securities and cash; weekends and holidays are not business days. Trade execution is a separate event.|Settlement exactly 24 hours after every trade.|Settlement one calendar month later.
capital26|Capital proposals in 2026|What was the status of the U.S. March 19, 2026 capital package when released?|Proposals for public comment, not automatically effective final rules.|The package addressed final Basel components, standardized capital and the GSIB surcharge. Keep proposals separate from later adopted rules and domestic implementation dates.|All Basel rules became globally identical that day.|Every requirement disappeared immediately.
stress26|September 2026 stress-test update|Did September’s final stress-test rules finalize the entire March capital package?|No: they were separate final rules with their own implementation timing.|The Fed finalized annual public input on scenarios and material model changes. Two-year stress-capital-buffer averaging begins in 2028. This is distinct from broader Basel capital proposals.|Yes, every March proposal took effect in September.|Yes, all stress testing ended.
private|Private credit|Why can infrequently marked private loans look less volatile than public debt?|Infrequent valuation can smooth reported returns without removing economic risk.|Borrower leverage, concentration, covenants, refinancing and illiquidity still matter. Compare cash flows and valuation methods before treating low reported volatility as safety.|Their prices cannot fall.|Private loans cannot default.
liquidity|Redemption versus asset liquidity|Why can a fund promising frequent redemptions while holding illiquid loans be fragile?|Investor cash demands can arrive faster than assets can be sold at fair value.|Gates, notice periods and liquidity buffers change the allocation of risk. Understand the actual redemption contract, not only the marketing label.|Illiquid assets can always be sold instantly at book value.|Redemption promises create extra asset value.
stable|Stablecoins|What should you examine before treating a stablecoin as dependable money?|Reserve quality, redemption rights, custody, governance and legal protections.|A target peg is not a guarantee. Mechanisms differ; asset-backed and algorithmic structures face different risks. Rules change and should be checked in the relevant jurisdiction.|Only whether the name includes stable.|Only the coin’s maximum supply.
token|Tokenization|Does tokenizing a bond eliminate its issuer’s credit risk?|No: the recording or transfer mechanism can change while the underlying obligation stays risky.|Rights, enforceability, custody and settlement design matter. A ledger entry is not automatically legal ownership of a redeemable asset.|Yes, all blockchain claims are risk-free.|Yes, it guarantees a constant market price.
crypto|Crypto custody|What special risk arises from self-custody of cryptoassets?|Loss or compromise of signing keys can make assets inaccessible or transferable by an attacker.|Exchange custody adds intermediary risk. Smart-contract bugs, governance and manipulation are other distinct hazards; a price history is not a security audit.|The issuer must restore every lost key.|Self-custody removes all operational risk.
ai|Backtesting and AI|Why can an impressive financial backtest fail in live use?|Overfitting, data leakage, costs and changing market relationships can undermine it.|Use genuinely out-of-sample data, realistic execution and careful validation. AI is a tool; adaptation by other traders can change the pattern it learned.|Past accuracy guarantees future returns.|A complex model cannot leak future information.
concentration|Index concentration|Does owning a capitalization-weighted index guarantee balanced economic exposures?|No: a few large firms or sectors can dominate its weights.|Count underlying exposures across sectors, countries, factors and liabilities. Many holdings do not imply equal weights or independence.|Yes, every index is equally weighted.|Yes, passive funds cannot be concentrated.
climate|Climate and insurance|Why can physical climate risks complicate insurance pooling?|They can change loss distributions and produce geographically correlated damage.|Insurance pricing, exposure growth, reinsurance capacity and adaptation affect coverage. Historical averages may understate future risks when conditions shift.|Climate risks are perfectly independent across neighboring houses.|More policies always eliminate catastrophe exposure.
cyber|Cyber and infrastructure concentration|Why can reliance on a shared critical service create systemic risk?|A common operational failure can disrupt many otherwise separate firms.|Payment systems, custody, cloud services and data feeds create dependencies. Redundancy, recovery plans and testing reduce but do not eliminate risk.|Each firm’s separate balance sheet prevents every shared outage.|Operational risk cannot affect financial markets.
''',kind='Current context')
